## 2. The two offers at a glance

| | **Origin** | **SUPA** |
|---|---|---|
| What it actually is | EV-ready **backbone** infrastructure | **Embedded-network** energy + hot-water supply |
| EV deliverable | Backbone for **every** car space (residents add own charger) | **3 shared** 3-phase chargers only |
| Headline cost to OC | **$125,000** indicative (firm quote available) | **$0** upfront — SUPA funds **$110,000** |
| What OC gives up | Money (one-off) | Energy/hot-water supply tie to a new operator for 10+ yrs |
| Asset ownership | OC owns the backbone | Unstated — likely SUPA owns meters + chargers |
| Energy lock-in | None | Yes — this is the whole point |
| Makes building EV-ready? | **Yes** (all bays) | **No** (3 chargers ≠ backbone) |

> **Pivotal context: the building is ALREADY on an Origin embedded network.** So the energy decision is **incumbent Origin vs challenger SUPA**, both embedded networks — not embedded vs open market. Retailer choice is already gone, so it's **neutral** between the two (don't argue it against SUPA). Origin wears two hats here: current embedded-network operator **and** the party quoting the $125k EV backbone. The single most important unknown is our **existing Origin contract** — its term and the exit/switching cost to hand the network to SUPA.

**The crux:** two different decisions are tangled together. (1) **EV-readiness** — Origin's backbone delivers it; SUPA's 3 chargers don't. (2) **Energy supply** — stay with incumbent Origin or switch to SUPA. They are separable: you can take Origin's backbone and still switch energy, or keep Origin energy and still do the backbone. Don't let one offer settle both, and don't compare SUPA's discounts to Origin's $125k capex.

